# Reference authoring profile

**Context:** the project-specific half of the reference methodology. The shared discipline
lives in `aidex-reference/references/` and does not change per project; **this file declares
only what varies here.** Materialized on {{DATE}} — it is meant to be edited, and a profile
nobody has edited is a profile nobody has applied.

> **If the stack is unfamiliar, do not guess the commands below.** Run an `aidex-research`
> spike first ("how is this stack laid out: routes, entry points, background work"), then fill
> this in. A wrong axis command reports full coverage of nothing.

---

## Census axes

Machine-read by `docs-census.py`. Each record is `axis` / `label` / `command`, blank-line
separated. The command runs from the project root and must print **one item per line**.

> **These are shell strings and they run as you.** The census refuses to execute a census block
> it has not seen approved on this machine (exit 3), printing the commands first; `--trust`
> approves that exact block and any edit to it revokes the approval. Approvals are stored under
> `$HOME`, never here, so this file cannot approve itself.

An axis returning zero lines is reported as **BROKEN**, never as "0 items, all covered" —
that would be a check that cannot fail.

```census
axis: routes
label: user-facing routes
command: grep -rhoE "path:[[:space:]]*'[^']+'" frontend/src/router --include=*.ts | sed -E "s/^path:[[:space:]]*'//; s/'$//" | grep -v "^$" | sort -u

axis: apps
label: installed backend apps
command: grep -oE "^[[:space:]]+'apps\.[a-z_]+" backend/config/settings/_base.py | sed -E "s/.*apps\.//" | sort -u
```

> **Use POSIX classes (`[[:space:]]`), never `\s`.** BSD `sed -E` on macOS does not honour
> `\s`, so a `\s`-based command silently fails to strip its prefix and every item comes out
> mangled — which the census then reports as 100% gap. Caught on this template's own first
> run, 2026-07-29.

